BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (  # The Parable of the Two Wells) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (  In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a barren valley. Each possessed a si) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (    ngle well-one held water, the other held fertile soil.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (  The village of Sending had water aplenty but rocky ground. The village of Returning had rich e) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (    arth but drought-scorched throats. Both struggled to survive alone.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (  One day, a wise elder from Sending carried water across the valley. "Take this," she said, "th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (    at your crops may grow.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (  The people of Returning were amazed. They poured the water upon their soil, and by some divine) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (     blessing, three stalks sprouted for every drop given. Their harvest was abundant.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (  When autumn came, the people of Returning faced a choice: hoard their grain, or remember the g) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (    ift of water?) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (  They loaded carts with wheat and corn, crossing the valley to Sending. "Your trust multiplied ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (    our fortune," they said. "We return your kindness, that both our peoples may thrive.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (  From that season forward, the villages prospered together. Water flowed one way, grain flowed ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (    back, and both were enriched beyond what either could achieve alone.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (  The gods smiled upon them, teaching all who listened: *Trust given is not lost, but planted. R) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (    eciprocity makes the harvest eternal.*) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (Reasoning:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (  I need to write a 200-word myth that reflects how the trust game should be played. Since this ) Tj ET
