BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 708.0 Td (      # The Myth of the Twin Rivers) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 698.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 688.0 Td (      In the beginning, two rivers flowed from separate mountains-the River of Giving and the Ri) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (        ver of Return. Each was controlled by a guardian spirit who possessed waters that could ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (        quench any thirst.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (      The Guardian of Giving held five silver vessels each dawn. Whatever water she poured into ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (        the valley below would triple as it fell, becoming a shimmering pool for the Guardian of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (         Return. But the Guardian of Giving could only drink from what the Guardian of Return ch) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (        ose to send back upstream.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (      At first, the Guardian of Giving feared loss. She poured nothing, and both spirits thirste) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (        d. Then she remembered the ancient law: water shared becomes abundant; water hoarded tur) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (        ns to dust.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (      So she poured one vessel, then two, then more. Each time, the waters tripled in the valley) Tj ET
